{
  "title": "nopCommerce \u2014 ecommerce (10,071\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: nopCommerce\nDomain: ecommerce | Scale: large | Stars: 10,071\nProblem: Businesses need a fully-featured, customizable, and free open-source eCommerce platform to build and run online stores of any size. nopCommerce provides a production-ready shopping cart solution built on ASP.NET Core with support for multiple databases and deployment environments.\nTarget users: Online retailers and store owners, eCommerce developers and agencies, Enterprises requiring customizable shopping platforms, Small and medium businesses launching online stores\nCore features: Product catalog management with categories, attributes, and variants; Shopping cart and checkout workflow; Admin area for store management and configuration; Multi-store and web farm support; Plugin/extension marketplace for third-party integrations; Multi-factor authentication and security features; REST Web API plugin for headless and mobile integrations; Multi-database support (MS SQL, PostgreSQL, MySQL)\nIntegrations: Microsoft Azure, Docker, MS SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, nopCommerce Marketplace (third-party plugins), REST Web API\nTech stack: ASP.NET Core MVC with Razor views, HTML, CSS, JavaScript + ASP.NET Core (.NET 9), C# + MS SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL (multi-database via ORM) + Docker, Azure, Web Farm support, cross-platform (Windows/Linux/Mac)\nKey libraries: ASP.NET Core MVC, Entity Framework Core, FluentMigrator (TSQL migrations), ASP.NET Core Identity, Async/await throughout\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Pluggable architecture with a marketplace-driven plugin system allows extending core functionality without modifying source code, enabling a large ecosystem of third-party integrations.\n- Full async implementation across all methods improves scalability and throughput for high-traffic eCommerce workloads.\n- Multi-database abstraction layer supports SQL Server, PostgreSQL, and MySQL, demonstrating the value of repository/ORM patterns for database portability.\n- Web farm and Azure compatibility is built-in from the ground up, indicating distributed-state management (e.g., distributed cache, shared sessions) was a first-class architectural concern.",
